# ── YOUR CONTENT NEVER GOES IN GIT ──────────────────────────────────────────
# board-data.json is the whole board: every card, wiki page and message you ever
# write. It is DATA, not source. If it is tracked, your repository becomes a
# permanent archive of your private content — and deleting something from the
# board does not remove it from git history, so every edited and deleted message
# stays recoverable by anyone who can clone.
#
# We learned this the expensive way: in the repo this project was extracted from,
# board-data.json had been tracked from day one and ended up inside more than half
# of all commits. That repository can never be published in any cleaned form. This
# line is the fix, and it has to be here before your first commit, not after.
board-data.json
*.bak
*.bak-*

node_modules/
.DS_Store
.claude/
backups/
attachments/
channel-config.json

# Debug/verification screenshots — throwaway artifacts, never committed
screenshot-*.png
Screenshot*.png

# Rendered wiki exports (#459) — generated per-share by export-wiki.mjs, never
# committed. The pipeline (export-wiki.mjs, core/export-*.mjs) and the rule table
# (EXPORT_TRANSFORMS.json) ARE source and are tracked; the HTML they produce is
# a disposable artifact of a specific moment. Committing it would also create a
# second copy of the doc that drifts from the wiki without anyone noticing.
*-export.html
architecture.html

# Your roster — who your people and agents are. Copy roster.example.json to
# roster.json and edit it. Kept out of git so a `git pull` never conflicts with
# who you are.
roster.json

# #503 — receipts and images are BOARD CONTENT, not code.
# The screenshot receipts this room generates (receipts-496/, receipts-499/…)
# are pictures of the live board: card titles, seat names, message text. A
# name-residue check smiles at a PNG. The convention was invented mid-sprint and
# lived only in the private workspace by placement discipline — one `cd` away
# from being pushable. Ignoring the surface is cheaper than remembering it.
receipts-*/
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp

# #1069 — ONE NAMED EXCEPTION, and it must stay one.
# The blanket image ignore above is a SAFETY RAIL, not tidiness. A screenshot
# of the board carries card titles, participant names and message text, and no
# text scan can read pixels — so the publication gate REFUSES a tracked image
# rather than passing it unjudged. That refusal is correct behaviour: "cannot
# judge" must never look like "passed".
# This one file was reviewed by a person, who ruled its visible content
# publishable on 2026-08-29. The exception IS that ruling, written down.
# ⛔ Do NOT broaden this to `!docs/*.jpg`. A per-file exception forces a human
#    ruling on every image; a directory exception forces none.
!docs/manyhands-commons.jpg

# #503 scope item 1, corrected: images were ALREADY blanket-ignored above
# (*.png et al — the "screenshot-*.png doesn't match" premise was false).
# The genuinely unprotected receipts content is the NON-image files
# (metrics.json carries board-derived data). This closes the directory.
receipts-*/

# #755 — the work-object log is DATA. It lives in the private workspace,
# never in the tree we publish. The rail is isGateArmed() refusing a store
# path inside the repo; this line is defence in depth.
work-objects/

# #805 — the tending provenance manifest names real people (who authored which
# prompt, who influenced its register). Source in this repo is public; the room's
# real identities live only in untracked data, as roster.json and board-data.json
# already do. tending-provenance.example.json IS tracked and carries synthetic
# seats. #804's graph-native pool retires this file.
tending-provenance.json
